WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy Award-winning actor,

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.637
you know from films such as,
"Field of Dreams,"

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.306
"Goodfellas,"
and "No Sudden Move."

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:09.308
He stars in the highly
anticipated "Sopranos" prequel,

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.544
"The Many Saints of Newark,"
which will be released

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:15.148
simultaneously in theaters and
on HBO MAX on October 1st.

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.649
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:21.087
-I'll tell you, Dick,
it's paradise over there.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:24.090
On the one hand,
you can't believe the beauty.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.660
On the other hand,
they don't even have toilets.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.063
I told you, 'Seppina, that
you're going to live the life

00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:33.399
of a proud American lady.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:37.102
And I'm going to have my second
set of children with you.

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:39.572
And they'll be deluged
with the life that they could

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.275
never even imagine over there.

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:46.912
Like you were, Dick, as a kid.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:49.215
These will be
your brothers and sisters.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.417
-Please welcome to the show,
Ray Liotta.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.186
♪♪

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:56.355
-[ Laughs ] Hey.
-How are you, man?

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:58.557
-Nice to see you, again.
How you doing?

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:01.294
-It's been awhile.
-It has been a long, long time.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:05.498
Come on up.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.766
-And we're supposed to fake
that there's people here?

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:09.436
-We -- no, no, no.
We're not going to --

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.205
we're just going to be honest
and say there's nobody here.

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.373
-I like it without.
-You do? Interesting.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:15.408
-Yeah.
-What do -- what do you

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.309
like about it?
-I don't know.

00:01:17.309 --> 00:01:19.979
Less pressure, less -- you know,
if you're saying something,

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.615
there's a joke.
There is no joke.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.983
If they're not laugh--
I don't know. I don't know.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:25.985
-I do -- it is a far more
natural conversation

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:27.420
without an audience.
-Totally. That -- yeah.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.588
-Yeah.
That beats -- as we adjust.

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:31.724
-This is -- I want to --
I want to ask,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.326
I'm so excited
about this movie.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.928
I've heard great things.
Have you seen it yet?

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:37.463
-I have.
-You have.

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:40.266
And are you happy with it.
-I -- I -- I am.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.935
It takes me a while, though,
when I --

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:46.239
to watch a movie that I'm in,
'cause I'm just looking at

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:49.375
so many different things.
-Are you very hard on yourself?

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.311
-I don't -- usually don't watch.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:52.345
-You don't like to watch
yourself?

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:53.946
-Yeah, no.
There's certain movies --

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:56.349
this I really wanted to see.
There's other directors

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.318
that I worked with, that,
obviously I wanted to see.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:02.021
But, in general, I -- I probably
only see less than half.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.457
-Now, later on,
if you're in a movie

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:07.360
that you don't see and you
run into, say, the director,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.062
do you admit to them that you
have not ever seen the movie?

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:12.398
-No. [ Laughs ]

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:14.600
Well, I would -- I -- see, we
would have gotten a good laugh.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.668
-Yeah. Right there you go.
-That's when you need them

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.869
there.
-There you go.

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:18.438
We -- we'll -- we'll make it
sound good later.

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:22.308
-Put it in. I would probably
avoid seeing the director,

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.376
or just say, "Nah, I haven't
seen it yet. I'm not sure.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:26.612
-Gotcha, gotcha.
This has been --

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:30.183
you have, kind of, been dancing
around "The Sopranos"

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.750
world for a while.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.754
Like, a lot of -- I think,
part of it is people

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.589
always wanted to see you
in it, because, you know,

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:39.458
"Goodfellas" is this iconic,
you know, film

00:02:39.458 --> 00:02:40.560
that's in the same world.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.529
I think, maybe, 27 actors
in "Goodfellas"

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:44.163
then went on to be
in "The Sopranos."

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.032
-[ Laughs ]
-It's some crazy number.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.200
And David Chase did approach you
about a part in

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.069
"The Sopranos," back
when the show was on TV.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:55.741
-Yeah. I was -- I was in
Virginia doing "Hannibal,"

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:59.111
and he came -- we had --
he took a train out.

00:02:59.111 --> 00:03:02.815
We -- we had lunch, and they
start telling me about the part.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:06.385
And I said, "Nah, I'm not sure.
I don't know.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:09.755
I'll let you know."
And for -- for --

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.591
who's the guy that got it?
-Joey Pants?

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.092
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.727
-Joey Pants got it
and won an Emmy.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.163
So, I made
the good move for him.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.798
-[ Laugh ]
-And the right move for me.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.233
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:23.369
And now, you find your way --
you find your way into the film.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.272
What -- David Chase --
I love watching David Chase

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:27.740
do interviews.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.909
I'm fascinated
to watch him talk.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:34.546
Seems very serious.
Is he -- is he fun to work with?

00:03:34.546 --> 00:03:37.082
-Huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.082 --> 00:03:38.918
I don't know if fun's the word.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.886
But, you know you're
in really good hands.

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:42.589
-Yeah.
-If things are going South.

00:03:42.589 --> 00:03:44.089
-Right.
He's just -- what I really liked

00:03:44.089 --> 00:03:47.893
about him, he's just very
intense and knows what he wants,

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.930
knows what he likes and is not
afraid to say it.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.100
-I'm assuming "The Sopranos" was
a show that you watched.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.270
And, like everybody who was
involved in the show,

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.306
and now who's involved in the
movie, like, you know,

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:03.042
you want to be careful with
something that has -- you know,

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.776
so well respected
and so well loved.

00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:06.779
Do you feel like everybody on
set knew they were --

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.848
they were, sort of, messing --
not messing with, but, like,

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:12.919
had to be delicate with
this beloved show?

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.753
-I never really watched it.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:15.888
-You didn't watch
"The Sopranos?"

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:16.989
-Shh.
-Okay, go on.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.357
[ Laughter ]
-No.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:19.892
The few episodes
in the beginning.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.428
But at that time, I just wasn't
into sitting home

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.796
and watching television.
-Yeah.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:27.499
-You were out doing stuff.
So, I mean, I -- I saw episodes.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:29.235
And, I said,
"Wow, this is really good."

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.936
Every now and then, you're
flicking and --

00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:33.939
you know, there's an episode
that's on.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:38.110
But no, I'm not -- I will.
I will, eventually.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.512
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.580
Well, now, the good news is,
since this is a prequel,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.415
you can watch it in order.
-Well, that's the other thing.

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:44.717
-Yeah.
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:45.818
-It didn't matter.
And I didn't have to sound like

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.253
anybody, because --
-Yes.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:49.155
-For some people, some of the
audience, some of the people

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:53.726
in our show go on to be on
the -- on the series.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:57.096
-So, in the clip, we saw
Alessandro --

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:58.197
how do you say his last name?
-Nivola.

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:01.033
-Nivola. You know,
I heard there's a --

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.835
there's a fight in the film.
And, obviously,

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.104
when actors fight, you try very
careful --

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:06.772
you try to be very careful, not
to actually make contact.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:08.674
-You'd think somebody would want
to be very careful.

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:10.843
-Yeah. But, I heard --
I heard Alessandro

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:14.413
clipped you a little bit.
-He did. He -- he did.

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:17.016
And I understand exactly where
he was coming from.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.684
This is like
a nice shot for him.

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:21.520
And he wants to make it
as real as possible,

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.656
but he got a little sloppy.
[ Light laughter ]

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.058
-So he hits you.
And then, I heard you --

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:27.927
you didn't let him off the hook.
You made him --

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:28.927
you made him sweat it
a little bit.

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:33.232
-[ Laughs ]
So, he called --

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.301
found out how --
see how I was doing.

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:38.805
And I answer the phone, and I
said, "Not very good, Jimmy."

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:42.041
What-- what -- I said, "You
broke my jaw."

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.510
And he really thought that I --
I kept it going.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.579
And, he, "Oh, my God, Ray, I'm
so sorry. I'm so sorry."

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:48.781
But, he didn't break it.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:49.982
But it was good to make him
sweat a little.

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:53.352
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
I think that's absolutely right.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:55.688
Hey, so we -- we last worked
together --

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.590
I think this may be the time I
saw you, 2003.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.759
-That's what -- yeah.
-2003, in this building,

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.061
you hosted "SNL."
And, I was actually texting with

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.228
my friend, Rachel Dratch, today.

00:06:03.228 --> 00:06:04.529
Which is why
it's funny to see you,

00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:06.432
because, this was, maybe,
for me,

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.100
the most memorable sketch you
did in the show.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.401
This was a kids' show.

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:11.204
And over the course
of the summer,

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:12.738
Rachel Dratch
was a kid who became,

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:14.106
maybe, a little bit
more of a woman.

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:16.141
-[ Laughs ]
-And you were --

00:06:16.141 --> 00:06:17.510
there was a lot of dancing
in the sketch.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:19.378
-Yeah.
-You were -- you were a --

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:20.646
-A kid show.
-It was a kid show.

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.281
-It was a kid show.
-You were the guy who

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:23.616
played the dinosaur,
and you were not --

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:26.952
you would not stand for it.
-Well, I -- I got the

00:06:26.952 --> 00:06:28.588
cigarette in my mouth now
and a drink.

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:33.559
So -- we taking a break. I think
Jimmy was -- was the director.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.095
And I'm upset. "Look at her."
I go, "How could you have her,

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:37.296
like, dancing like that?"
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:39.632
-Because when she danced,
things just --

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:40.932
-Yeah. Things happen.
-Go.

00:06:40.932 --> 00:06:42.434
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]
-And they went.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.770
It was a really good --
-I thought it was a --

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.338
-Tina Fey wrote that.
Were you part of that?

00:06:46.338 --> 00:06:48.507
-I was only -- only just to
watch and enjoy.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:50.476
But, I thought it was an
excellent use of --

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:53.512
of Ray Liotta.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:55.080
Hey, congrats on the movie.
-Thank you.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.883
-I'm so excited to see it.
There have been very few

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.784
movies I've been
anticipating this much.

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:00.353
And it's just
great to see you again.

00:07:00.353 --> 00:07:01.687
-Oh, good.
I hope we don't disappoint you.

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:03.255
-[ Laughs ]
"The Many Saints of Newark"

00:07:03.255 --> 00:07:05.191
is in theaters and on HBO MAX
on October 1st.

